{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqgu5ovs60x","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"Retrieval is a DAG edge read; RAG runs at node-write time and is materialized into context_deps","summary":"Zonoid does NOT retrieve at use time. The RAG pipeline (hybrid maxCosine -> cross-encoder -> structBoost -> context gate in routes/graph.js, plus the autowire / suggestForTask paths in daemon.js) runs at NODE-WRITE time: it generates and judges candidates, then persists the winners as context_deps edges. At use time a fully-judged (non-provisional) task reads those edges at score 1.0 with no embed / cosine / rerank — the dagOnly branch at routes/graph.js:166. Only provisional / un-wired nodes fall back to live RAG to fill slots. This is easy to misread: tracing /search shows a live RAG scoring pipeline, but that is the write-time candidate generator (and the interactive search_knowledge surface), NOT steady-state retrieval. Consequence 1: the field-level multi-vector (.vecs) upgrade improves write-time candidate quality (better edges persisted), not the DAG read, which has no scoring to upgrade. Consequence 2: the injection/context gate (lib/context-gate.js) has an ASYMMETRIC loss unlike normal RAG — a false inject is durable (a bad edge is frozen into the DAG and read forever, never re-judged), so it must be precision-biased, whereas a false abstain is recoverable via later writes.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T16:07:43.672Z","vec":[-0.15324047207832336,0.022529400885105133,-0.03359350189566612,0.04698218032717705,0.024913078173995018,-0.08943970501422882,-0.026737654581665993,0.07697184383869171,0.010928000323474407,-0.024201082065701485,-0.04703730344772339,0.060524746775627136,0.02730005420744419,-0.00959633570164442,-0.03441304340958595,0.01225866936147213,0.1176094263792038,0.03498575836420059,-0.0203557126224041,-0.06416038423776627,0.05877010524272919,0.011329421773552895,0.030748628079891205,-0.0881374254822731,0.025956543162465096,-0.005900813732296228,-0.